er can treat a matching local-only tag as satisfied, violating the required remote-tag proof.\\n- **Verification**: Delete/migrate every sibling observer and test that a matching local-only tag is `Absent`, while only an exact remote annotated tag is `Exact`.\\n\\n### High: Tests do not exercise the production observer\\n\\n- **Status**: CONFIRMED\\n- **Location**: `release_observation.rs:1035-1224`\\n- **Classification**: Spec Drift\\n- **Root Cause**: No test calls `observe_prepared`, `observe_registry`, `observe_forge_publish`, or `fetch_github_release` through a validated intent/artifact capability. The “supported gh argv” test manually reconstructs the expected argv and invokes the runner directly, so a regression in production argv would still pass. Exact, Absent, Incomplete, Conflict, Unverifiable, tag substitution, asset mismatch, registry mismatch, and cache invalidation are not tested end to end.\\n- **Verification**: Build a validated fixture and drive every operation variant through `observe_prepared`, asserting calls and all five outcomes.\\n\\n### Medium: npm observation reads untrusted tarballs into memory before checking expected length\\n\\n- **Status**: CONFIRMED\\n- **Location**: `release_observation.rs:844-906`, called from `observe_registry:369+`\\n- **Classification**: Boundary Condition\\n- **Root Cause**: `fs::read` allocates the entire registry-provided tarball; only afterward does the caller compare length. A broken or hostile registry can return a multi-gigabyte file for the approved version and exhaust the process.\\n- **Verification**: Pass expected length into the helper, require a single `Component::Normal`, compare file metadata first, then stream the digest. Test an oversized response without allocating it.\\n\\nResolved during review:\\n\\n- Every observation now rechecks workspace trust and prepared remote identity.\\n- Cache invalidation exists for post-effect observation.\\n- npm absence requires structured `E404`/`ETARGET`.\\n- 